   "parsing_res_list": [
    {
     "block_label": "header",
     "block_content": "Coatings 2024, 14, 137",
     "block_bbox": [
      141,
      205,
      455,
      239
     ],
     "block_id": 0,
     "block_order": null
    },
    {
     "block_label": "paragraph_title",
     "block_content": "2. Materials and Experimental Methods ",
     "block_bbox": [
      683,
      367,
      1442,
      406
     ],
     "block_id": 1,
     "block_order": 1
    },
    {
     "block_label": "paragraph_title",
     "block_content": "2.1. Materials and Experiment Setup ",
     "block_bbox": [
      684,
      421,
      1321,
      464
     ],
     "block_id": 2,
     "block_order": 2
    },
    {
     "block_label": "text",
     "block_content": "The material used in this study was the second-generation nickel-based superalloy DD6, and all materials were obtained from Beijing Institute of Aeronautical Materials in China. DD6 is widely employed in turbine working blades and guide vanes due to its outstanding overall performance. The elemental composition of DD6 is shown in Table 1.",
     "block_bbox": [
      680,
      486,
      2327,
      689
     ],
     "block_id": 3,
     "block_order": 3
    },
    {
     "block_label": "figure_title",
     "block_content": "Table 1. Elementary composition (wt%) of single-crystal alloy DD6.",
     "block_bbox": [
      683,
      749,
      1799,
      789
     ],
     "block_id": 4,
     "block_order": null
    },
    {
     "block_label": "table",
     "block_content": "<html><body><table><tr><td>Elements</td><td>Cr</td><td>Al</td><td>Mo</td><td>Co</td><td>W</td><td>Ta</td><td>Re</td><td>Ni</td></tr><tr><td>wt%</td><td>3.8~4.8</td><td>5.2~6.2</td><td>1.5~2.5</td><td>8.5~9.5</td><td>7.0~9.0</td><td>6.0~8.5</td><td>1.6~2.4</td><td>Bal</td></tr></table></body></html>",
     "block_bbox": [
      145,
      830,
      2326,
      964
     ],
     "block_id": 5,
     "block_order": null
    },
    {
     "block_label": "text",
     "block_content": "Before laser processing, the specimens were cut into 30 mm × 10 mm × 0.8 mm pieces (Figure 1) using wire-cut electrical discharge machining followed by grinding and polishing using an ultra-precision lapping machine (LAP-1X, Lab, Shanghai, China) Subsequently,the samples were immersed in an alcohol solution for ten minutes before drilling. After drilling, the surface and pore wall morphology results of air film pores were observed using field emission scanning electron microscopy(MIRA3, TESCAN, Brno, Czech Republic).",
     "block_bbox": [
      680,
      1043,
      2329,
      1350
     ],
     "block_id": 6,
     "block_order": 4
    },
    {
     "block_label": "image",
     "block_content": "",
     "block_bbox": [
      694,
      1411,
      1771,
      2064
     ],
     "block_id": 7,
     "block_order": null
    },
    {
     "block_label": "figure_title",
     "block_content": "Figure 1. Experimental sample topography.",
     "block_bbox": [
      683,
      2112,
      1407,
      2155
     ],
     "block_id": 8,
     "block_order": null
    },
    {
     "block_label": "text",
     "block_content": "The schematic of the ultrafast laser processing used in this experiment is depicted in Figure 2. A commercially diode-pumped solid laser (PH1-20, Light Conversion, Vilnius,Lithuania) was used in this work, thus emitting pulse laser with a wavelength of 1030 nm,a pulse width of 290 fs, a maximum power of 20 W, and a repetition frequency of 100 kHz.During the machining process, the workpiece was securely fixed on a 5-axis machining platform via a fixture. The workpiece's status was continuously monitored through a CCD camera. When workpiece penetration was detected, it indicated the completion of machining, and the process was stopped.\n",
     "block_bbox": [
      679,
      2200,
      2331,
      2615
     ],
     "block_id": 9,
     "block_order": 5
    },
    {
     "block_label": "paragraph_title",
     "block_content": "2.2. Experimental Design ",
     "block_bbox": [
      684,
      2661,
      1129,
      2705
     ],
     "block_id": 10,
     "block_order": 6
    },
    {
     "block_label": "text",
     "block_content": "Laser drilling typically employs three methods: percussion drilling, ring cutting, and spiral trepanning [27]. Repeated impact drilling involves single-point impulses that abrade the center of the specimen, thereby resulting in poor machining quality and difficulties in controlling hole diameter. Concentric circular drilling can lead to difficulties in removing material from the hole as the feed depth increases. On the other hand, spiral driling continuously processes microholes by starting from the inside and working downwards,thereby resulting in superior processing quality compared to impact drilling and concentric circle drilling. Therefore, this paper investigated the impact of laser process parameters (laser power, defocus, feed distance, and scanning velocity) on the geometrical accuracy ",
     "block_bbox": [
      679,
      2727,
      2328,
      3193
     ],
     "block_id": 11,
     "block_order": 7
    },
    {
     "block_label": "number",
     "block_content": "3 of 16",
     "block_bbox": [
      2227,
      213,
      2326,
      245
     ],
     "block_id": 12,
     "block_order": null
    }
   ],
